From bc2b46c2f4e0ae7b204b6d5d2cb373569d5f8efe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Amp <amp@ampcode.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 18:56:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Runtime: keep each unwind's state when cleanups throw, and
 per fiber

The unwind state (context, exception, found frame, forced-unwind callback)
lives in filc_thread, and the compiler's resume does not pass the exception
back. Two ways to have more than one unwind in flight broke it:

- A cleanup landing pad (a destructor) that throws and catches its own
  exception overwrote the outer unwind's state. The outer resume then went on
  with the inner exception, which __cxa_end_catch had already freed, and the
  next landing pad's personality call trapped reading a freed object. Now the
  inner raise saves the outer state when phase 1 finds its handler (in a chain
  of runtime-private objects, filc_thread.saved_unwind_state_ptr), and the
  handler's landing pad restores it if the frame running the outer cleanup is
  still live, or clears the state. The failed-phase-1 paths also no longer
  clobber is_force_unwinding.

- Fibers: boost coroutine2 rethrows a coroutine's exception in the caller,
  whose cleanup destroys the coroutine, which switches back and unwinds the
  fiber with forced_unwind. The per-thread state mixed the two stacks' unwinds
  (filc_resume_unwind asserted found_frame_for_unwind). swapcontext now moves
  the unwind state into the context being left, and switching to a context
  restores its own, as native code gets for free by keeping the state in the
  exception and on the stack.

Found by Nix's functional tests (read-only-store, binary-cache,
multiple-outputs-substitute-failure) and nix-util-tests' decompress test.

Amp-Thread-ID: https://ampcode.com/threads/T-01a0e2af-4054-707b-8968-337622993848
Co-authored-by: Mikael Brockman <mikael@brockman.se>
